+// The read-through window (eden_guest_read_through_copy): open while the pool is near its end, shut
+// 10 s after it was last: a write of the game's memory waits for a block, the reserve is under half,
+// or the pool's free memory in all is under 160 MiB (EDEN_CRAM_READ_THROUGH_MIB; read every 500 ms,
+// eden_pool_free_bytes in headless/performance.cpp). Its largest free block says nothing here: the
+// pool is in small pieces (median 4 MiB) all session, so the window was open nearly always (below).
+// Only while a write waited, it opened too late for a load: the castle area's graphics reads
+// unpacked slots into the last blocks, and GPUBack's own write back then found none (22 Nov 00:19). Another PS5 port got Ocarina of Time through its load
+// from the field into the next area 6 of 6 times with it, against 0 of 2 to 3 of 4 without; open all
+// the time it cost 4 to 6 fps. It was open while the pool was short at all (the reserve under half,
+// or the largest free block under 256 MiB), which in these games is nearly always: 1.2-1.7 million
+// reads a session, a million of them unpacking a whole 2 MiB slot on a graphics thread, and the run
+// with more of them (core 0132) met its wall sooner with twice the refused images (21 Nov).
+// EDEN_CRAM_READ_THROUGH=wide opens it as before, =wait only while a write waits.
+extern "C" [[gnu::weak]] std::uint64_t eden_pool_free_bytes(void) noexcept;
+std::atomic<bool> read_through_open{false};
+
 // ---- The 64-bit fastmem window's aliases (src/host_memory.cpp, Window64; backported from ProsperoEden) ----
